title: A month without a single crisis
new-page: true
on-arrival: {!
const quietMonthKey = Q.year + "-" + Q.month;

const quietMonthHeadlines = {
    "2019-11": "The new Sejm discovers that opposition arithmetic has two chambers",
    "2019-12": "The restored Left turns parliamentary presence into an organisation",

    "2020-1": "The presidential field forms before the campaign finds its subject",
    "2020-2": "The presidential campaign struggles for oxygen",
    "2020-6": "The delayed presidential campaign returns under epidemic rules",
    "2020-8": "The alliance counts its losses before the merger",
    "2020-12": "Europe closes one budget fight as the vaccination winter begins",

    "2021-2": "Vaccination and the published Tribunal ruling reshape winter politics",
    "2021-4": "The recovery fund divides the governing coalition",
    "2021-6": "The Polish Deal forces every opposition party to publish its own arithmetic",
    "2021-7": "KO resets its leadership and compresses the opposition field",
    "2021-12": "The governing majority survives another year by postponing its arguments",

    "2022-1": "The Polish Deal reaches payslips before its authors agree on an explanation",
    "2022-3": "Municipal aid networks become part of national politics",
    "2022-6": "KPO approval turns a European dispute into a milestone dispute",
    "2022-10": "Energy support enters every budget and every campaign promise",
    "2022-12": "The war budget leaves little room for an ordinary political winter",

    "2023-1": "The election year begins without an agreed opposition route to power",
    "2023-3": "Farm prices turn European solidarity into a domestic distribution fight",
    "2023-6": "Street mobilisation tests which opposition can convert attendance into votes",
    "2023-8": "The election date hardens every coalition negotiation",
    "2023-11": "Parliamentary arithmetic replaces campaign arithmetic",
    "2023-12": "A cabinet inherits institutions shaped by earlier governments",

    "2024-1": "The transfer of power becomes a struggle over state authority",
    "2024-3": "Local lists expose the limits of national coalition unity",
    "2024-6": "The European election redraws leverage inside every Polish alliance",
    "2024-7": "Winning office has not settled the coalition programme",
    "2024-11": "The presidential nomination becomes the next coalition stress test",

    "2025-1": "The presidential field turns every government dispute into campaign evidence",
    "2025-3": "Candidates compete to define what the parliamentary transition achieved",
    "2025-7": "The presidential result redistributes leverage before the summer recess",
    "2025-8": Q.president_name + " begins a term shaped by the parliamentary majority",
    "2025-9": "The first budget after the presidential election tests cohabitation",
    "2025-11": "Every party reads the presidential result as a different mandate",

    "2026-1": "The government enters the KPO deadline year with little slack left",
    "2026-3": "Local organisations behave as if the parliamentary campaign has begun",
    "2026-6": "The coalition argues over what can still be finished before election year",
    "2026-8": "A quiet summer fills with reshuffle rumours and candidate arithmetic",
    "2026-10": "The 2027 list negotiations begin before anyone admits they have begun",
    "2026-12": "The last full pre-election budget becomes a manifesto in appropriations",

    "2027-1": "The parliamentary election year begins before the formal campaign",
    "2027-3": "Constituency bargains turn national alliances into local arguments",
    "2027-5": "Manifestos promise four years while leaders negotiate four months",
    "2027-7": "The campaign becomes a contest over ownership of the entire term",
    "2027-9": "Every parliamentary vote is now campaign material",
    "2027-11": "The election result creates another coalition arithmetic problem",
    "2027-12": "Coalition talks decide which campaign promises become conditions"
};

Q.quiet_month_has_copy = quietMonthHeadlines[quietMonthKey] ? 1 : 0;
Q.news_headline =
    quietMonthHeadlines[quietMonthKey] ||
    "No single story owns the political month";
!}

= [+ date_label +]

[? if year = 2019 and month = 11:
The Left returns to the Sejm after four years outside it. A common electoral
list must now become a parliamentary organisation, while the opposition's
unexpected Senate strength creates a second arena for every institutional
fight. ?]

[? if year = 2019 and month = 12:
Committee assignments, parliamentary staff and the first legislative
initiatives expose the practical meaning of unity. SLD, Wiosna and Razem can
share a podium more easily than authority, money or responsibility. ?]


[? if year = 2020 and month = 1:
The Left must choose what kind of presidential campaign it intends to run
before voters have decided what the election is about. Every prospective
candidate promises independence while looking nervously at the coalition
parties behind them. ?]

[? if year = 2020 and month = 2:
[+ presidential_candidate +]'s campaign tours a country already watching the
first reports of a new virus. KO fights over its own nomination; Hołownia
builds an outsider movement; the Left's message competes with both. ?]

[? if year = 2020 and month = 6:
The presidential campaign returns after the abandoned May vote, now compressed
into several weeks and governed by epidemic precautions. Organisation,
television access and confidence in the electoral process matter almost as
much as the candidates. ?]

[? if year = 2020 and month = 8:
The presidential result has redistributed prestige but settled nothing. SLD
and Wiosna prepare their legal merger while Razem asks whether unity still has
a programme. ?]

[? if year = 2020 and month = 12:
The European budget compromise removes one immediate threat while vaccination
logistics become the next test of state capacity. The opposition must criticise
the government without making administrative failure sound desirable. ?]


[? if year = 2021 and month = 2:
Vaccine access grows while the abortion judgment enters force. Public health
and the rights conflict now share many of the same exhausted organising
networks. ?]

[? if year = 2021 and month = 4:
Solidarna Polska opposes the EU recovery mechanism. PiS begins looking for
opposition votes; every opposition party calculates who will receive the money
and who will be blamed for the bargain. ?]

[? if year = 2021 and month = 6:
The Polish Deal attempts to convert government spending into a new political
settlement. The Left must distinguish its redistributive demands from PiS
policy without defending every tax interest threatened by the package. ?]

[? if year = 2021 and month = 7:
A leadership reset inside KO changes the balance of the opposition without
solving its strategic disputes. Smaller parties must decide whether renewed
polarisation creates bargaining power or merely consumes their electorate. ?]

[? if year = 2021 and month = 12:
The governing camp has survived another year of internal rebellions without
resolving them. Inflation, public services and institutional conflict enter
the winter as separate problems competing for the same political attention. ?]


[? if year = 2022 and month = 1:
The Polish Deal reaches payroll systems, accountants and household budgets.
Technical corrections arrive faster than political explanations, giving every
party an opportunity to defend some taxpayers while quietly sacrificing
others. ?]

[? if year = 2022 and month = 3:
Railway stations, schools, private homes and municipal offices form an
improvised reception system for people fleeing Ukraine. Local government and
civil society acquire responsibilities that national politicians will soon
try to claim or redistribute. ?]

[? if year = 2022 and month = 6:
European approval of Poland's recovery plan does not itself release an
unconditional stream of money. The argument moves from whether KPO exists to
which milestones are real, who must implement them and who will own the result.
?]

[? if year = 2022 and month = 10:
Energy prices enter municipal budgets, household bills and industrial policy
at the same time. Temporary shields are politically immediate; the ownership,
heating and investment choices beneath them are not. ?]

[? if year = 2022 and month = 12:
Defence, refugee support and energy intervention harden the state budget before
the election year begins. Every expensive commitment appears permanent to its
beneficiaries and temporary to the finance ministry. ?]


[? if year = 2023 and month = 1:
The election year begins with several theories of opposition cooperation and
no accepted method for choosing between them. Arguments about one list,
separate lists and future coalition discipline become substitutes for the
campaign itself. ?]

[? if year = 2023 and month = 3:
Ukrainian agricultural imports turn a foreign-policy consensus into a domestic
distribution conflict. Farmers ask who bears the cost of solidarity; parties
answer according to the constituencies they hope to keep. ?]

[? if year = 2023 and month = 6:
Large demonstrations show the opposition's capacity to mobilise anger but not
how that support will divide at the ballot box. Each party wants the image of
unity without surrendering its reason to exist. ?]

[? if year = 2023 and month = 8:
The election date turns provisional alliances into immediate negotiations
over lists, districts and campaign resources. The accompanying referendum
fight gives the government and opposition competing ways to define what the
vote is about. ?]

[? if year = 2023 and month = 11:
The election has produced parliamentary arithmetic, not yet a governing
practice. Coalition agreements, presidential procedure and control of the
Sejm calendar now matter as much as the promises made during the campaign. ?]

[? if year = 2023 and month = 12:
A cabinet assumes responsibility for a state still staffed, funded and legally
constrained by earlier choices. The Left must decide which conflicts require
an immediate rupture and which demand patient institutional control. ?]


[? if year = 2024 and month = 1:
The first weeks after government formation turn appointments, public media and
prosecutorial authority into tests of legitimacy. Restoring institutional
control and restoring institutional rules are not always the same operation. ?]

[? if year = 2024 and month = 3:
Local-election lists expose disputes that national coalition agreements could
temporarily conceal. Mayors, councillors and regional organisations care less
about parliamentary choreography than places on lists and control of local
budgets. ?]

[? if year = 2024 and month = 6:
The European election measures enthusiasm only months after the parliamentary
transfer of power. A low-stakes national contest becomes a referendum on
coalition identity, opposition recovery and the usefulness of smaller parties.
?]

[? if year = 2024 and month = 7:
The coalition has won office but not a common theory of government. Social
spending, rights legislation and institutional repair each create different
majorities, veto points and definitions of acceptable compromise. ?]

[? if year = 2024 and month = 11:
Presidential nominations become proxy contests over the coalition's direction.
Every party wants a candidate capable of winning the runoff and sufficiently
dependent on the people who helped them reach it. ?]


[? if year = 2025 and month = 1:
The presidential field turns every cabinet dispute into evidence about the
next head of state. Candidates promise to rise above party conflict while
their campaigns measure every word against the electorate of the first round.
?]

[? if year = 2025 and month = 3:
The campaign asks voters to judge what the parliamentary transition actually
changed. Government parties advertise delivery, opposition parties advertise
obstruction, and the Left must explain which achievements genuinely bear its
imprint. ?]

[? if year = 2025 and month = 7:
The presidential result redistributes prestige across the political system.
Victors describe a mandate; defeated parties discover strategic lessons that
conveniently support arguments they already intended to make. ?]

[? if year = 2025 and month = 8:
[+ president_name +] begins a new term with formal powers, electoral promises
and an immediate relationship to the parliamentary majority. Appointments and
veto threats acquire more weight than the ceremonial language of inauguration.
?]

[? if year = 2025 and month = 9:
The first budget after the presidential election tests whether government and
head of state intend cooperation, selective bargaining or permanent conflict.
Lewica is judged not only by what it proposes but by which compromises it
makes possible. ?]

[? if year = 2025 and month = 11:
Every party reads the presidential result as proof that its preferred
realignment is necessary. The same electorate is described as demanding
moderation, mobilisation, unity and ideological clarity. ?]


[? if year = 2026 and month = 1:
The government enters the final KPO deadline year with less administrative and
political slack than its public statements suggest. Unfinished projects now
compete with the budget, coalition maintenance and preparations for 2027. ?]

[? if year = 2026 and month = 3:
Local organisations begin recruiting candidates, protecting incumbents and
counting viable constituencies. National leaders continue to deny that the
parliamentary campaign has started while making decisions that only make sense
if it has. ?]

[? if year = 2026 and month = 6:
The coalition argues over which reforms can still be completed before election
year and which should remain useful promises. Ministers defend their own
projects; parties defend the achievements they expect voters to remember. ?]

[? if year = 2026 and month = 8:
The parliamentary recess produces no legislation but an abundance of
reshuffle rumours, leadership briefings and candidate-list arithmetic. A quiet
summer is merely political conflict conducted without recorded votes. ?]

[? if year = 2026 and month = 10:
Negotiations over the 2027 lists begin through deniable conversations about
coordination, incumbency and electoral efficiency. Every prospective alliance
is presented as a matter of principle after its district arithmetic has been
checked. ?]

[? if year = 2026 and month = 12:
The last full pre-election budget operates as a manifesto written in
appropriations. Programmes funded now can be presented as delivery; programmes
scheduled later remain promises whose authorship will be contested. ?]


[? if year = 2027 and month = 1:
The parliamentary election year begins before the formal campaign. Government
parties defend a record, opposition parties assemble an indictment and every
internal faction asks whether loyalty will earn places on the lists. ?]

[? if year = 2027 and month = 3:
Constituency negotiations turn national alliances into local conflicts.
Parties that agree on the prime minister can still disagree fiercely over the
second viable position in a medium-sized district. ?]

[? if year = 2027 and month = 5:
Manifestos promise four years of coherent government while party leaders
negotiate the next four months of campaign coordination. Expensive commitments
multiply before anyone is required to reconcile them. ?]

[? if year = 2027 and month = 7:
The campaign becomes a contest over ownership of the entire parliamentary
term. Coalition partners claim its popular achievements individually and
assign its compromises to the government collectively. ?]

[? if year = 2027 and month = 9:
Every parliamentary vote is now campaign material. Bills are judged by their
contents, their coalition consequences and the thirty-second clip each party
expects to publish afterward. ?]

[? if year = 2027 and month = 11:
The election result replaces campaign arithmetic with coalition arithmetic.
Parties that spent months defining one another as unacceptable begin producing
careful distinctions between surrender, negotiation and national
responsibility. ?]

[? if year = 2027 and month = 12:
Coalition talks decide which campaign promises become governing conditions,
which become commissions and which disappear into explanatory footnotes. The
Left's leverage depends on seats, alternatives and its willingness to use
both. ?]


[? if quiet_month_has_copy = 0:
No single shock dominates the month. Ministers administer, parties organise
and local structures prepare for conflicts that have not yet acquired a
national headline. ?]

- @poland_hub: Return to the leadership table